 87	\subsection{Polynomials over finite fields}
 88	
 89	\begin{definition}[Polynomials of low individual degree]
 90	Let $q$ be a prime power,
 91	and let $m$ and $d$ be nonnegative integers.
 92	We define $\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$ to be the set of polynomials in $\F_q^m$
 93	with individual degree~$d$.
 94	\end{definition}
 95	
 96	\begin{remark}
 97	Note that as defined in \Cref{sec:intro},
 98	in this work a polynomial with individual degree~$d$ is one in which the degree~$d_i$ of each coordinate~$i$
 99	is \emph{at most~$d$}.
100	This allows us to say ``individual degree~$d$'' rather than the wordier ``individual degree at most~$d$''.

